From 9e51c69d731532c52387b7554ebac0bdda1c9b2c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 05:05:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 09/11] fix(gateway): recover panics with an error envelope
 that carries ids

Recovery is the outermost middleware, so it never saw the correlation
id or the trace id that Correlation and TraceContext put into the
downstream context: every "panic recovered" line had an empty corr=
and no trace, and the 500 was a plain-text body without either id.

Recovery now puts a small holder into the request context that
Correlation and TraceContext fill in. The panic log carries both ids
and, while no header has been written, the response is the
INTERNAL_ERROR envelope with the same ids. WriteError and the
recovery path share writeEnvelope, which also drops a Content-Length
the handler may have set before panicking and sets nosniff as
http.Error did.

Once headers are written, the status and the bytes already sent
(usually from the control plane or an instance) cannot be changed.
Recovery used to write "Internal Server Error" after them, which a
client cannot tell apart from the real body. It now logs the panic
and re-panics with http.ErrAbortHandler so that net/http aborts the
response, and it passes a handler's own http.ErrAbortHandler
(httputil.ReverseProxy uses it when copying a body fails) through
without logging it as a panic. Observe and AccessLog record from a
deferred call, so aborted requests are still counted and logged.
